\section{Introduction}
    The following document is an educational project made for the course \textit{Parallel Programming For Machine Learning}. Although the title suggests that the arguments involved do not threaten any Machine Learning algorithm, it underlines the difficulty and differences in parallel implementations. \\
    
    The authors of the document do not assume any responsibility for illegal uses of the provided software, especially for the LM Hash Cracker. \\

    The LAN Manager is a protocol created by Microsoft and used in the 80s, initially included in the \textbf{SMB} protocol. However, it still appears in many Active Directory protocols such as LDAP and Kerberos, for legacy and older-standard compatibility reasons. If some years ago this was not a real problem, today it is. In fact, with modern hardware, the old algorithm underlines some weaknesses that can be successfully exploited to recover up to a 14-byte-long password in a bunch of minutes, depending on the hardware. Full weaknesses and algorithm will be discussed later. 
    Fundamentally, LAN Manager relies on the LM hash, a cryptographic function designed to store and authenticate user passwords. While Microsoft eventually introduced the more secure NT LAN Manager (NTLM) and later adopted Kerberos as the primary authentication protocol, the LM hash was retained for decades to ensure backward compatibility with older clients like Windows 95 and Windows ME. Although modern Windows operating systems; starting with Windows Vista and Server 2008; disable LM hash storage by default, misconfigured legacy environments or specific backward-compatibility requirements often inadvertently leave this attack surface open. LM hash cracker implemented is a \textbf{pure brute-forcer} program which scans the entire space-set of all combinations possible, it does not require any external wordlist or any other input apart from the hash itself. \\

    On the other hand, we also implemented the Bloom Filter as the second project argument. The Bloom filter has completely different challenges, which involve more memory-bound operations than CPU-bound operations. 
    Bloom Filters are space-efficient data structures that are primarily used as a mean to check if an element is part of a set. Since it is space-efficient, it is usually used with a great volume of data (e.g during a sign in it is used to check if the given password is acceptable or not). \\
    Generally, a Bloom Filter is an array of bits (could also be a boolean or byte array) initialized to 0. To insert an element, the element first needs to be converted into indices through the use of \textbf{k} hash functions, returning k indices. Once converted, the positions in the bit array indicated by the indices are set to 1 (this will lose all information about the element). The check works in the same way: the element to be searched is converted into indices, and then the Bloom Filter checks whether the value stored in those positions is 1 or 0. If all positions contain 1s, the element is part of the set; otherwise, if even one 0 is present, the element is not part of the set. \\

    We want to underline that although the 2 project arguments were reviewed by the other partner, we organized our work by splitting the tasks in:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item \textbf{BloomFilter} \textit{implemented by Roberto}
      \item \textbf{LM Hash Cracker} \textit{implemented by Angelo}
    \end{itemize}

    The project source code and compilation directives for each can be found at \href{https://github.com/Virgula0/ProgettoPPM}{GitHub Repository}

