    \subsection*{Hardware}
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{CPU}: Intel Core i5-12600K (10 Physical Cores: 6 Performance-cores with Hyper-Threading + 4 Efficient-cores, for a total of 16 Threads [12 managed by P-Cores via Hyper-Threading + 4 managed by E-Core])
            \item \textbf{GPU}: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 Ti (Ampere Architecture, 4,864 CUDA Cores)
            \item \textbf{RAM}: 32 GB DDR4 @ 3000 MHz
        \end{itemize}
        
    \subsection{Algorithm and Challenges Meet}
        The LM Hash algorithm is not difficult to understand, but still difficult to implement especially in its CUDA version, since the hash requires the official \textit{DES NIST fips 46-3} implementation \href{https://csrc.nist.gov/files/pubs/fips/46-3/final/docs/fips46-3.pdf}{https://csrc.nist.gov/f\\iles/pubs/fips/46-3/final/docs/fips46-3.pdf}, which has been \textbf{fully re-implemented from scratch} since nothing about this version in CUDA has been found online already working. Trying to use OpenCL kernels by hashcat, required more adaptation than expected so the decision was to proceed for a full re-implementation. \\

        The algorithm proceeds as follows:

        \begin{enumerate}
            \item The user's password is restricted to a maximum of fourteen characters
            \item The user's password is converted to uppercase, this reduces the total combinations to test from $95^7$ to $69^7$, a big degradation.
            \item The user's password is encoded in the System OEM code page. This step has actually been ignored because we will work with ASCII characters only.
            \item The password is NULL-padded to 14 bytes
            \item The “fixed-length” password is split into two 7-byte halves. This is the real problem of this standard. Even with 69 characters, with a full 14 characters bruteforce the total amount of combinations would be $69^{14}$, which even with an obsolete algorithm like DES behind, would be computationally infeasible for consumer hardware in a reasonable time.
            \item These values are used to create two DES keys, one from each 7-byte half, by converting the seven bytes into a bit stream with the most significant bit first, and inserting a parity bit after every seven bits (so 1010100 becomes 10101000). This generates the 64 bits needed for a DES key. (A DES key ostensibly consists of 64 bits; however, only 56 of these are actually used by the algorithm. The parity bits added in this step are later discarded.)
            \item Each of the two keys is used to DES-encrypt the constant ASCII string \textbf{KGS!@\#\$\%} resulting in two 8-byte ciphertext values. The DES CipherMode should be set to ECB, and PaddingMode should be set to NONE.
            \item These two ciphertext values are concatenated to form a 16-byte value, which is the LM hash.
        \end{enumerate}

        The proposed charset is composed of all printable characters and mostly matches those used as the full charset \textit{?a} in hashcat, excluding the lowercase charset as they are converted by the algorithm in uppercase.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=c]
char FULL_CHARSET[] = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789!\"#$" \
"%&'()*+,-./:;<=>?@[\\]^_`{|}~ ";
\end{lstlisting}

    \subsection{Search Space Calculation}
        For a representative password of length $L = 14$ (e.g.\ \texttt{TEST123TEST123}), the nominal total number of candidate passwords is:
        
        \begin{equation}
        C(14) = 2 \times 69^{7} = 14{,}892{,}706{,}505{,}178 \approx 1.489 \times 10^{13} \quad (\approx 14.9 \text{ trillion candidates})
        \end{equation}
        
        \subsection{Peak GPU Throughput}
        We derive the peak throughput $H_{\text{peak}}$ of our custom CUDA implementation from the $L=7$ measurement of \texttt{TEST123} ($T = 37.00\text{ min} = 2{,}220\text{ s}$, $C(7) = 69^{7} = 7{,}446{,}353{,}252{,}589$):
        
        \begin{equation}
        H_{\text{peak}} = \frac{C(7)}{T} = \frac{7{,}446{,}353{,}252{,}589}{2{,}220} \approx 3.354 \times 10^{9} \text{ hashes/second} \quad (\approx 3.35 \text{ GH/s})
        \end{equation}
        
        This value matches the throughput we independently observe across all rows of Table~\ref{tab:performance_comparison} ($L=7$–$13$, $\approx 3.35$~GH/s). All of these measurements are taken over relatively short executions (well under an hour), and are therefore unaffected by sustained GPU thermal throttling; we adopt $H_{\text{peak}}$ as the device's short-run peak rate.
        
        \subsection{True Worst-Case Search Space and Sustained Runtime}
        \label{sec:worst_case}
        Because \texttt{indexToCandidate} enumerates the charset in a fixed order, the genuine worst case is not merely the longest password, but the one whose candidate index is enumerated \emph{last}. Since the space character (\texttt{0x20}) occupies the final position (index 68) of \texttt{FULL\_CHARSET}, a 14-character password composed entirely of spaces forces an exhaustive search over \emph{all} lengths $L=1,\dots,7$ for \emph{both} LM halves:
        
        \begin{equation}
        C_{\text{worst}}(14) = 2\sum_{L=1}^{7} 69^{L} = 15{,}111{,}716{,}894{,}958 \approx 1.511 \times 10^{13} \text{ candidates}
        \end{equation}
        
        We measured this worst-case password twice, under identical code (S-boxes kept in global \texttt{\_\_device\_\_} memory in both cases, as throughout the rest of the implementation), differing only in whether the two LM halves were processed concurrently on two CUDA streams or sequentially on a single stream:
        
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{Concurrent (two CUDA streams):} $T_{\text{streamed}} = 15{,}549.4711 \text{ s} \approx 4.319 \text{ hours}$
            \item \textbf{Sequential (halves processed one after another):} $T_{\text{sequential}} = 14{,}914.6760 \text{ s} \approx 4.143 \text{ hours}$
        \end{itemize}

        The sequential version without stream can be found on the branch: \href{https://github.com/Virgula0/ProgettoPPM/tree/lm-hash-cracker-without-stream-usage}{lm-hash-cracker-without-stream-usage}.
        The two runtimes differ by only $4.08\%$, ruling out significant inter-stream resource contention. Since the concurrent implementation is architecturally simpler and shows no measurable penalty, we retain it in our final implementation and adopt its measurement as the reference worst-case runtime:
        
        \begin{equation}
        T_{\text{GPU\_custom}} = 15{,}549.4711 \text{ s} \approx 4.319 \text{ hours}
        \end{equation}
        
        From this measurement we obtain the sustained throughput:
        
        \begin{equation}
        H_{\text{sustained}} = \frac{C_{\text{worst}}(14)}{T_{\text{GPU\_custom}}} = \frac{15{,}111{,}716{,}894{,}958}{15{,}549.4711} \approx 9.72 \times 10^{8} \text{ hashes/second} \quad (\approx 0.97 \text{ GH/s})
        \end{equation}
        
        We attribute the difference between $H_{\text{peak}}$ ($\approx 3.35$~GH/s, short runs) and $H_{\text{sustained}}$ ($\approx 0.97$~GH/s, the multi-hour worst-case run) to GPU boost-clock throttling under prolonged thermal and power load: the worst-case run keeps the device at $100\%$ utilization for over four continuous hours, versus well under an hour for every other measurement in Table~\ref{tab:performance_comparison}, consistent with typical consumer GPU power-management behavior. Thermals reached $78^\circ$ degrees in the long run which is pretty high considering the used device architecture. \\
        
        As supporting evidence, we compare the representative case (\texttt{TEST123TEST123}, total work $6.816 \times 10^{12}$ candidates, $T \approx 4{,}386$–$4{,}439\text{ s}$) against the worst case (total work $1.511 \times 10^{13}$ candidates, $T = 15{,}549.4711\text{ s}$). The workload increases by $2.22\times$, while the runtime increases by $3.55\times$. The exhaustive length 1–6 phase, common to both, takes nearly identical time in each case ($224.86\text{ s}$ vs.\ $228.23\text{ s}$, a $1.5\%$ difference), which localizes the additional slowdown to the length-7 phase; precisely the portion of the run long enough for thermal throttling to become measurable.
        
        \subsection{Throughput Metrics}
        Using the sustained worst-case throughput and its associated CPU projection:
        
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{Custom CUDA Implementation:}
            \begin{equation}
            \text{Throughput}_{\text{GPU\_custom}} \approx 9.72 \times 10^{8} \text{ hashes/second} \quad (\approx 0.97 \text{ GH/s})
            \end{equation}
        
            \item \textbf{OpenMP (CPU) Implementation (projected):}
            Using the empirically measured CPU throughput of $56.08\text{ MH/s}$ (Table~\ref{tab:performance_comparison}) applied to the same worst-case search space $C_{\text{worst}}(14)$:
            \begin{equation}
            T_{\text{OpenMP}} = \frac{15{,}111{,}716{,}894{,}958}{56.08 \times 10^{6}} \approx 269{,}467.1 \text{ s} \approx 3.119 \text{ days}
            \end{equation}
            \begin{equation}
            \text{Throughput}_{\text{OpenMP}} \approx 5.608 \times 10^{7} \text{ hashes/second} \quad (\approx 56.08 \text{ MH/s})
            \end{equation}
        
            \item \textbf{Hashcat (Reference):}
            Provided for context only and excluded from the speedup comparison below, since we benchmarked Hashcat independently rather than running it to completion on the same search space. Empirically measured throughput on the same hardware: $\text{Throughput}_{\text{Hashcat}} \approx 30 \text{ GH/s}$, roughly $31\times$ our sustained worst-case throughput, thanks to advanced kernel-level optimizations (bit-slicing and advances table storage and management) that fall outside the scope of this project.
        \end{itemize}
        
        \subsection{Speedup and Time Saved}
        Comparing our custom CUDA implementation against its OpenMP (CPU) counterpart on the true worst-case search space, using $S = \dfrac{T_{\text{OpenMP}}}{T_{\text{GPU\_custom}}}$:
        
        \begin{equation}
        S = \frac{269{,}467.1 \text{ s}}{15{,}549.4711 \text{ s}} \approx 17.33\times
        \end{equation}
        
        The percentage of time saved by our GPU implementation over the CPU implementation, $E = \max\left(0, \left(1 - \frac{1}{S}\right) \times 100\right)$:
        
        \begin{equation}
        E = \left(1 - \frac{1}{17.33}\right) \times 100 \approx 94.23\%
        \end{equation}
        
