    \subsection{CUDA GPU Version - in-depth technical analysis}

    As anticipated, the implementation in \textbf{CUDA} of the algorithm is not that straightforward, so for more complex technical parts (for example, eventually explaining the \textbf{DES} implementation details) we rely on specific questions during the oral interview. Here, instead, it will follow in a more general way how the code is organized and the rationale behind the decisions made. \\

    The algorithm firstly performs an optimization checks, (please note that these checks are also performed by the OpenMP version): since we're assuming to crack password and not raw bytes, it is assumed to be impossible to have the second 7 bytes of the password not empty if the first 7 bytes are all null bytes (\textbf{0x00}). Then, another optimization is performed which checks, with higher priority, if the second half of the password is composed by only null bytes, if yes, it is assumed that the password is less or equal than 7 character, so the flag to skip the computation of the second half of the password is set to 1, and the stream initialization is completely skipped. \\

    Threads number per block are dynamically asked to the GPU using the function \textbf{cudaOccupancyMaxPotentialBlockSize} and here the first real bug solved. For passwords 7 bytes long, the algorithm at the end used to print out empty passwords. This was a pretty strange behavior and clearly a bug. The fact is that the bug was caused by the \textbf{MaxGrid dimension} (2147483647 in our case). In fact with 7 characters, the total computation for a password exceeded the max grid dimension of the GPU, resulting in a silent failure from the cuda streams. The solution here was to divide the number of total passwords ($69^7$), assigning to each cuda stream the total combinations of passwords if the number of combinations is less than the chunkSize dimension (which corresponds to the MaxGrid dimension multiplied by the number of threads per block); otherwise we calculate the number of chunks for splitting the work and we pass the maximum assignable amount of work (which is equals to chunkSize) for each chunk. \\

    As someone might object, by looking at the code, it does not make too much sense to use 2 streams, assigning each one to crack half of a password. This is because, when running a single stream, the function \textbf{crackHalfKernel} already uses 100\% of the cores of the GPU. This means that having 2 streams running and computing the same size will double the required time to crack the hash. This is true especially because streams are helpful for in-memory or not so expensive ALU bounded operations, but here, our algorithm just relies on math tasks, which do not require particular scheduling overhead. The real reason why the cracking was implemented using 2 streams is that this is more elegant in the code, at least in our opinion. After all, the alternative would have to iteratively proceed on the first half of the hash and then, when the first one has been cracked, eventually proceed on the second half of the password (you can actually still find this implementation in the branch \href{https://github.com/Virgula0/ProgettoPPM/tree/lm-hash-cracker-without-stream-usage}{lm-hash-cracker-without-stream-usage}). This is practically the same amount of time and computation; assigning the work to the 2 streams results in not only a more elegant solution, but also relies entirely on the GPU scheduling and in-memory optimizations for the few bytes to swap in and out from threads' registers. Not only this, assuming that the first half is, for example, 7 bytes long and the second part is 5 bytes long, with 2 streams working parallelly, the second half is anyway cracked in few seconds thus, once the second half is cracked, threads and resources are automatically released and re-assigned for the first stream, which since by our assumption is computing a 7 bytes long password, it is surely still processing. So, in summary, the usage of the streams is not only more elegant but actually useful whenever the two 7 bytes halves of the password have different lengths among each other. \\

    The remaining interesting function that may need attention is how thread-to-candidate mapping works. The function which performs such operation is \textit{indexToCandidate} implemented as follows:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=c]
__device__ void indexToCandidate(uint64_t index, int len, char* out_str) {
    // Da destra a sinistra
    for (int i = len - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        // Carattere in base alla posizione corrente del thread
        out_str[i] = FULL_CHARSET[index % CHARSET_SIZE]; 
        index /= CHARSET_SIZE; // Avanza alla posizione successiva
    }
    out_str[len] = '\0'; // terminatore stringa, restituisci il candidato
}

__global__ void crackHalfKernel(
    const uint8_t target_bytes[8],
    char* cracked_out,
    uint64_t start_index,
    uint64_t total_work,
    unsigned int candidate_len,
    unsigned int* found_flag
) {
    // Calcolo dell'ID globale del thread
    // blockDim.x = 768 come suggerito da cudaOccupancyMaxPotentialBlockSize
    // perche' ogni blocco composto da 768 threads
    // blockIdx.x = id del blocco
    // threadIdx.x = posizione generica di un dato thread nel blocco 
    uint64_t gid = blockIdx.x * (uint64_t)blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    uint64_t idx = start_index + gid; // rappresenta l'id reale e univoco del thread

    // Senza idx con password da 7 byte si ha un bug:
    // la dimensione della griglia eccede il max.
    // Soluzione: usare chunks.
    if (idx >= total_work || *found_flag != 0) {
        return; // Guard closure
    }

    // Calcola il candidate. Allocazione fissa a 8 byte 
    // per evitare allocazioni dinamiche sullo stack.
    char candidate[8];
    indexToCandidate(idx, candidate_len, candidate);

    // Encrypt candidate
    uint8_t candidate_block[8];
    hash_half_fast(candidate, candidate_len, candidate_block);

    // Check against known target
    if (compareEqualBytes(candidate_block, target_bytes)) {
        if (atomicExch(found_flag, 1u) == 0) {
            // Copia il candidato trovato nel buffer di output
            for (unsigned int i = 0; i < candidate_len; i++) {
                cracked_out[i] = candidate[i];
            }
            cracked_out[candidate_len] = '\0';
        }
        return;
    }
}
\end{lstlisting}

    This is the core of the real difference between the CPU version (OpenMP) and the CUDA GPU version. The candidate is calculated dynamically by processing the index thread number and mapping it numerically to the candidate thanks to the fixed charset size. The \textbf{atomicExch} guarantees that only 1 thread will copy the found candidate into the output buffer. The \textbf{start} position is calculated based on the current chunk assigned to the kernel, over the total calculated in the main loop. The standard computation of \textbf{gid} and \textbf{idx}, which can be found in most CUDA kernels (1d size), gives spatial information to find the specific thread to assign the computation; it maps the grid into one single continuous array. \\

    One last observation to make regarding this part is the treatment of the DES permutation tables. In fact, in the \textbf{des.cu} it can be seen that all permutation tables are stored in the constant memory using the \textbf{\_\_constant\_\_} directive, but storing the \textbf{S\_BOX} lookup table in the same way, so in the constant memory, resulted in drastically reducing the performance of the cracking speed, but not only this; in fact, with this minor change, the usage of the GPU cores rarely reaches 100\%, even with a full intensive cracking task on a 7-byte half. We actually don't know the real reason behind this, but we can hypothesize that one reason is that the S\_box returns different index results for each password guess. Since each password guess is performed by a different thread, the attempt to access a smaller memory like \textbf{\_\_constant\_\_} is fully serialized in atomic operations, since its large dimension exceeds the optimal cache line size. So the problem could be the fact that the large number of threads are trying to access the same memory portion at the same time simultaneously.
        
    \subsection{OpenMP CPU Version - in-depth technical analysis}
        Regarding the OpenMP version, technically there is not that much to say; the most interesting part was the parallel implementation of the algorithm. In fact, to create the parallel version, we first created the iterative normal one on the CPU to better study the algorithm. After that, the only changes made were adding pragma directives to parallelize the operations. Notice the parallelized code is practically the same as the CUDA version, allowing a real one-to-one comparison between the two. \\

        In fact, via the OpenMP \textbf{section} directives, the two halves of the hash are split and manipulated concurrently. Actually, in OpenMP, there is also another OpenMP parallelisation which is performed on different lengths. As can be noticed, the scheduling of the threads is dynamic, leaving OpenMP in auto mode regarding the thread scheduling directives and management. Although it is the simplest implementation, the speed of $56.08 MH/s$ is not so irrelevant for a CPU. Regarding the optimizations, they're the same as the already discussed null-byte checks optimizations performed by the CUDA version. There is only one method which is really different and is useful to underline; this is \textbf{build\_combo\_dfs}. The method is interesting because the CUDA version, as already explained, uses a map based on thread indexing for calculating the next candidate. Here instead, which version was written actually before the CUDA implementation, uses a \textit{Depth-First Search} algorithm for calculating the next candidate, since calculating $69^7$ of candidates not only would take probably more of the time of the cracking itself, but would eventually lead the dynamic or the stacked memory to explode due to the huge dimensions of the full combinations. Here follows some maths that better underlines this concept:

        \section*{Pre-generated Candidate List}
        \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{T}_{\text{list}} = \mathcal{O}(N^L)
        \end{equation*}
        \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{S}_{\text{list}} = \mathcal{O}(L \cdot N^L)
        \end{equation*}
        
        Standard iterative approach that pre-generates and stores all possible candidates in memory before evaluation.
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $N = |\Sigma|$ (alphabet size, e.g., 69)
            \item $L$ = maximum password length (7)
        \end{itemize}
        
        \section*{Depth-First Search (DFS)}
        \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{T}_{\text{dfs}} = \mathcal{O}(N^L)
        \end{equation*}
        \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{S}_{\text{dfs}} = \mathcal{O}(L)
          \end{equation*}
        
        Recursive traversal that generates and tests candidates on-the-fly, reclaiming memory via backtracking.
        
        \section*{Key Differences}
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{Time Complexity ($\mathcal{T}$):} Identical ($\mathcal{O}(N^L)$). Both methods evaluate the exact same exponential number of total combinations ($K = \sum_{k=1}^{L} N^k$).
            \item \textbf{Space Complexity ($\mathcal{S}$):} The pre-generated list requires \textbf{exponential space} ($\mathcal{O}(L \cdot N^L) \approx 61\text{ TB}$ of RAM for $L=7$), making it unfeasible. DFS reduces this to \textbf{linear space} ($\mathcal{O}(L)$, a few bytes on the stack).
        \end{itemize}

        Another important difference is the use of OpenSSL to encrypt the candidate with the DES algorithm. In fact, if in CUDA the \textbf{des.cu} represents a real re-implementation of the DES, instead here, we use the OpenSSL library which actually should not be a problem, the opposite instead! In fact, it is uncommon to rewrite cryptographic algorithms for security and performance reasons, so given that this is a dedicated library whose purpose is to implement standard crypto algorithms, we can be quite sure it is better than any custom DES implementation. The \textbf{des\_encrypt\_block} is the function which is used to encrypt the current candidate block, and it can be seen that it initializes and frees the OpenSSL directives each time is called. This is the only overhead included in the code, in contrast to the GPU version, but since using a single shared context could lead to undefined behavior, we decided to accept this small difference.

        \newpage
            
\section{Bloom Filter Parallelized}        
    \subsection{Compilers, Framework and Hardware Used}
        The testing iterations of both implementations have been conducted on the same machine, with the following specifications of interest:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{CPU}: AMD Ryzen 7 3750H (8 physical threads) with x86-64 architecture.
            \item \textbf{RAM}: 2 x 8GiB SODIMM DDR4 Synchronous Unbuffered 2667 MHz
            \item \textbf{CACHES}: 384KiB L1 cache, 2MiB L2 cache, 4MiB L3 cache.
        \end{itemize}
        The following compilers have been used in this work:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{C++ Compiler}: g++ --version | g++ (Ubuntu 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1) 13.3.0 Copyright (C) 2023 Free Software Foundation, Inc.
            \item \textbf{Python Version}: Python 3.13.15 GIL free
        \end{itemize}
        The frameworks, libraries and modules utilized are:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{OpenMP}, with pragma directives.
            \item \textbf{MurmurHash3}, used to optimize hashses calculations.
            \item The module \textbf{ThreadPool} form the library multiprocessing, to achive true multithreading on Python.
        \end{itemize}

     \subsection{Algorithm and Challenges Meet}
        The objective of this work is to understand how much improvement is achieved using parallelization techniques and how much it differs between programming languages. 
        For this work, two programming languages have been analyzed: C++ and Python, with OpenMP and pragma directives for the C++ implementation and with a Python free-threaded version (an experimental version that allows deactivating the GIL and achieving real multithreading via thread pooling) for the second implementation.
        The challenges faced in this work are, firstly to understand how parallelization works in this two different environments, secondly, to understand what parts of the Bloom Filter algorithm are parallelizable, and lastly, to gather the data needed to make an educated consideration on the parallel implementations.\\

        To compare sequential and parallel performances, both implementations are divided into two parts: in one part of the implementations, the operations and algorithms are done sequentially, while in the other part, the operations use parallelisation techniques. Also, to correctly compare the two implementations, both of them must follow the same strategies used for parallelisation (for example, if one implementation utilises a shared byte array that uses a synchronisation mechanism to allow threads to safely write in the byte array, then the other implementation must utilise the same kind of shared byte array that uses the same kind of synchronisation mechanism). \\

        Most of the challenges met during the making of this part of the assignment came from the Python implementation, because in Python the possibility of true multi-threading (parallelism), as opposed to the traditional GIL-limited threading (concurrency only), is being introduced only in the experimental free-threaded build of Python 3.13; this means that the base and constructs for parallelisation on Python are not as streamlined and optimised as the ones present in C++. This means greater overhead in thread management and in memory access management, in addition to making it more difficult to keep the Python implementation coherent with the C++ implementation. \\

        Both implementations proceed as follows:

        \begin{itemize}
            \item load the passwords from the files \textit{rockyou.txt} and \textit{parole\_uniche.txt}.
            \item populate the dictionary using the \textit{add} method.
            \item checks if the passwords of \textit{parole\_uniche.txt} are present inside the dictionary, using the \textit{contains} method.
        \end{itemize}

    \subsection{Performance Attributes}
        Firstly, we'll need to address the problem of false positives. In a Bloom filter, there cannot be false negatives due to the property of the Hash functions, but there can be false positives; this is caused by the fact that processing a password and, with the indices obtained, changing the bits indicated by the indices to 1s, loses any information about the password and, with the finite dimension of the bit array, the indices generated for a new password could coincide with bits already set to 1 by previously inserted passwords. The following formula describes the probability of having false positives:

        $$p = \left(1 - \left[1 - \frac{1}{m}\right]^{kn}\right)^{k}$$ or approximated $$p = \left(1 - e^{\frac{-kn}{m}}\right)^{k}$$

        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{m} is the number of bits in the byte array (dynamically calculated and measured in bits).
            \item \textbf{k} is the number of hash functions required for processing the passwords.
            \item \textbf{n} is the number of elements of the dictionary.
        \end{itemize}
        
        With this formula, we can calculate the probability of having false positives.
        Taking into account the fact that we are working on a dictionary with 14 million passwords, even a probability of $2\%$ can result in roughly 280'000 false positives. To minimise this, we set our acceptable threshold below $1\%$ and selected parameters to achieve a target probability of $0.8\%$.
        In our test case we assigned $\textbf{m} = 143443770$, $\textbf{k} = 6$, $\textbf{n} = 14344377$, obtaining the following probability:
        $$p = \left(1 - \left[1 - \frac{1}{143443770}\right]^{6*14344377}\right)^{6} = 0.00845$$
        So, we predict that the expected number of false positives when querying 999997 passwords in our test case will be around 8,449, which is acceptable for the scope of this test. The predicted number of false positives will also be useful for checking the correctness of the Bloom Filters implementation.
        \\
        The metrics used for the performance comparison, are:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{Time to Complete (TTC)}: the execution time measured for both sequential and parallel implementations of the Bloom Filter operations.
            \item \textbf{Speedup}: indicates how much faster the parallel implementation is relative to the sequential implementation, measured with the formula: $speeedup = \frac{TTC_{sequential}}{TTC_{parallel}}$
            \item \textbf{Efficiency}: quantifies how effectively a parallel algorithm utilizes the allocated hardware resources (processing cores or threads). It measures the proportion of processing power contributed by additional processors toward accelerating execution. It is obtained by the following formula $efficiency = \frac{speedup}{numberOfThreads}$
        \end{itemize}

        These metrics will be used internally to each version to compare the two implementations, as well as externally to compare the C++ version with the Python version.
        \newpage
    \subsection{C++ OpenMP Version - in-depth technical analysis}
        We began by implementing an abstract Bloom Filter that will be used as a base for the parallel and sequential implementations by encapsulating all the shared methods. \\
        Before continuing with the methods description, it is important to note that the environment variables \textit{OMP\_PROC\_BIND} and \textit{OMP\_PLACES} values have been changed to \textit{OMP\_PROC\_BIND=spread} and \textit{OMP\_PLACES=threads}. This modifications basically tells the Operating System to schedule the threads on hardware threads rather than hardware real cores (because of Hyper-threading) and that thread distance between each other is one. This combination enforce thread pinning improving performances. \\ \\
        The abstract class has two public methods:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{void add(const std::string\& item)}, takes a password and its length as inputs, and passes them to the \textit{\_hash} method that returns two 64-bit hashes; these hashes are then passed to \textit{\_hash\_single} method that, with the Double Hashing technique, returns \textbf{k} indices that are used by the method \textit{\_set\_bit} to set the bits indicated by the indices to 0 or 1.
        \end{itemize}
        It also defines four protected methods:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{std::array$<$uint64\_t, 2$>$ \_hash(const uint8\_t* data, std::size\_t len)}, Takes a password and its size as input, then calculate the 128 bit hash of the password, using MurmurHash3, one time and returns the tuple (h1, h2) formed by the two 64bits parts of the hash through an \textit{uint64\_t} array with two positions.
            \item \textit{size\_t \_hash\_single(uint64\_t h1, uint64\_t h2, size\_t hash\_idx)}, this method takes as inputs two 64 bits hashes (the two parts of the 128 bit returned by \textit{\_hash}) and an index of \textit{size\_t} type; using the Double Hashing technique it returns the index of the position in the byte array where to put one.
            \item \textit{void \_set\_bit(size\_t bit\_index)}, Takes an index as input, because of the bit-packing technique the method elaborates the index so that it points at the right bit in the right byte, and then set the bit to 1.
            \item \textit{bool \_get\_bit(size\_t bit\_index)}, Does the same operations as the method \textit{\_set\_bit} but instead of setting to 1 the indicated bit, it returns \textbf{true} if the bit is equal to 1, otherwise it returns \textbf{false}.
        \end{itemize}
        \begin{lstlisting}[language=c++]
void add(const std::string& item) {
    auto [h1, h2] = _hash(reinterpret_cast<const uint8_t*>(item.data()), 
        item.size());
        
    for (size_t j = 0; j < K_HASHES; ++j) {
        _set_bit(_hash_single(h1, h2, j));
    }
}
        \end{lstlisting}
        \begin{lstlisting}[language=c++]
bool contains(const std::string& item) const {
    auto [h1, h2] = _hash(reinterpret_cast<const uint8_t*>(item.data()), 
        item.size());
        
    for (size_t j = 0; j < K_HASHES; ++j) {
        if (!_get_bit(_hash_single(h1, h2, j))) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
        \end{lstlisting}
        \begin{lstlisting}[language=c++]
std::array<uint64_t, 2> _hash(const uint8_t* data, std::size_t len) const {
    std::array<uint64_t, 2> hashValue;
    MurmurHash3_x64_128(data, len, 0, hashValue.data());
    return hashValue;
}
        \end{lstlisting}
        \begin{lstlisting}[language=c++]
size_t _hash_single(uint64_t h1, uint64_t h2, size_t hash_idx) const {
    return static_cast<size_t>(h1 + hash_idx * h2) % size;
}
        \end{lstlisting}
        \begin{lstlisting}[language=c++]
void _set_bit(size_t bit_index) {
    bit_array[bit_index >> 3] |= static_cast<uint8_t>(1u << (bit_index & 7));
}
        \end{lstlisting}
        \begin{lstlisting}[language=c++]
bool _get_bit(size_t bit_index) const {
    return (bit_array[bit_index >> 3] &
            static_cast<uint8_t>(1u << (bit_index & 7))) != 0;
}
        \end{lstlisting}

        Both the sequential and parallel implementations override the virtual methods defined in the abstract base class:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{void add\_from\_file(const std::vector$<$std::string$>$\& items)}
            \item \textit{size\_t contains\_from\_file(const std::vector$<$std::string$>$\& items)}
        \end{itemize}
        
        These methods are used to insert or check large batches of passwords from a file (roughly 14 million passwords in \textit{rockyou.txt} and around 1 million in \textit{parole\_uniche.txt}).
        In the parallel implementation, these methods are parallelized using \textit{OpenMP} \textit{pragma} directives. These two methods are the only feasible candidates for parallelization, because the nested for loops are small and run only a few times per password. Parallelizing these loops would only introduce severe context-switching and thread management overhead while parallelizing the external for loops avoids re-initializing threads on every cycle.

        \begin{lstlisting}[language=c++]
void add_from_file(const std::vector<std::string>& items) override {
#pragma omp parallel num_threads(NUMBER_OF_THREADS)
#pragma omp for schedule(dynamic, 1024)
    for (size_t i = 0; i < items.size(); ++i) {
        auto [h1, h2] = _hash(reinterpret_cast<const uint8_t*>
        (items[i].data()), items[i].size());
        for (size_t j = 0; j < K_HASHES; ++j) {
            size_t bit_index = _hash_single(h1, h2, j);
            
            size_t byte_index = bit_index >> 3;
            uint8_t mask = static_cast<uint8_t>(1u << (bit_index & 7));

#pragma omp atomic update
            bit_array[byte_index] = bit_array[byte_index] | mask; 
        }
    }
}
        \end{lstlisting}
        As previously mentioned, the external for loop of the \textit{add\_from\_file} method is executed, in this test case, around 14 million times, making the this loop the primary objective for parallelization. So, the directive \textbf{\#pragma omp parallel} is used to set the number of threads that will be utilized, using the clause\\ \textit{num\_threads(NUMBER\_OF\_THREADS)} with value \textit{omp\_get\_max\_threads()} allows us to utilize all available CPU cores, so that different machines can avoid the overhead of thread management.
        We then use the \textit{\#pragma omp for} directive to divide the loop iterations among the threads. We set the scheduling policy to \textbf{dynamic} with a chunk size of \textbf{1024}, which dynamically balances the workload across threads to improve performances. We also tried to use the \textbf{collapse} clause to parallelize the inner for loop, but the use of this clause introduces overhead since for every external loop task, six additional tasks are created for the inner loop (so about 84000000 tasks in total) increasing thread switching and management overhead. \\
        A race condition arises from the use of the bit-packing technique, because when simultaneous write operations are performed on the same byte will cause the last write operation to overwrite the previous operation and as a consequence, corrupts the byte. The solution that we choose is to use the \textit{\#pragma omp atomic update} directive to make sure that the write operation on the byte array cannot be interrupted and so ensuring their safety (the use of this directive introduces overhead, but it is a necessary evil).
        
        \begin{lstlisting}[language=c++]
size_t contains_from_file(const std::vector<std::string>& items) const override {
    size_t count = 0;
#pragma omp parallel num_threads(NUMBER_OF_THREADS)
#pragma omp for schedule(dynamic, 1024) reduction(+ : count)
    for (size_t i = 0; i < items.size(); ++i) {
        if (contains(items[i])) {
            count++;
        }
    }
    return count;
}
        \end{lstlisting}
        The \textit{contains\_from\_file} method is lighter than \textit{add\_from\_file}, and only has one for loop making it quite easy to parallelize. Its parallelization strategy is almost the same as in the \textit{add\_from\_file} method, the only meaningful difference is that we used the clause \textbf{reduction(+ : count)} on the \textit{\#pragma omp for} directive, so that the thread-local results are safely accumulated into the count variable, correctly returning the total number of passwords found in the dictionary. Notice that, unlike the \textit{add}, the \textit{contains} operation does not perform \textit{write} operations on it and thus does not need any atomic directive for loading bits from the bit array, and threads can read the same portions of bits from the same byte safely. This drastically simplifies the entire operation, avoiding any lock and obtaining a significant speed-up improvement, much more meaningful than the add; this will be noticeable later in performance analysis.

    \subsection{Python ThreadPooled version - in-depth technical analysis}
        To ensure a fair performance comparison between the C++ and Python implementations, the Python script was designed as a direct translation of the C++ implementation. This required adopting memory structures that closely match those used in C++, using equivalent parallelization strategies, and maintaining identical control flows across methods.
        The script follows the structure of its C++ counterpart in the following ways:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item it uses an abstract Bloom Filter class containing the implementations of the shared methods \textit{add}, \textit{contains}, \textit{\_set\_bit}, \textit{\_get\_bit}, \textit{\_hash\_single} and \textit{\_hash}.
            \item the method \textit{\_hash} uses the same non-cryptographic hash function, MurmurHash3, and returns the same two 64 bit hashes; the \textit{\_hash\_single} also uses Double Hashing to calculate the indices associated to the passwords. 
            \item it uses a byte array of the same dimension, and the same bit-packing strategy to store the dictionary.
            \item It uses the exact same benchmark datasets (\textit{rockyou.txt} to build the dictionary and \textit{parole\_uniche.txt} to evaluate false positives).
            \item It executes the same number of test iterations and evaluates the same performance metrics.
        \end{itemize}
        The parts of the Python script that are radically different from its C++ counterpart (obviously ignoring the differences intrinsic to the programming language itself) are in the methods \textit{add\_from\_file} and \textit{contains\_from\_file} in the parallel version of Bloom Filter.
        The most glaring difference is the \textit{get\_next\_chunk} method:
        \begin{lstlisting}[language=python]
def _get_next_chunk(self, total_items: int) -> tuple[int, int] | None:
    with self._sched_lock:
        start = self._next_index
        if start >= total_items:
            return None
        end = min(start + DYNAMIC_CHUNK_SIZE, total_items)
        self._next_index = end
        return start, end
        \end{lstlisting}
        The purpose of this method is to emulate the dynamic scheduling of the parallelized for loops in the C++ implementation. It basically takes the array of strings containing the passwords extracted from \textit{rockyou.txt} and using the \textbf{DYNAMIC\_CHUNK\_SIZE} (in our test case it's equal to 1024) returns the boundary indices of the chunk that a given thread must process.
        \\
        The  differences between \textit{add\_from\_file} and \textit{contains\_from\_file} with their C++ counterparts are caused by the different way in which Python manages threads and multithreading.
        Because of this both methods, rely on inner methods: methods (\textit{worker()} for \textit{add\_from\_file} and \textit{process\_chunk(chunk)} for \textit{contains\_from\_file}). These methods are assigned and executed by the threads and contains the execution logic, while the parent method handles the creation and lifecycle management of the threads. Aside from this structural adaptation, the inner methods faithfully replicate the logic of their C++ counterparts.
        \begin{lstlisting}[language=python]
def add_from_file(self, items: list[str]) -> None:
    self._next_index = 0
    total_items = len(items)

    def worker():
        while True:
            chunk = self._get_next_chunk(total_items)
            if chunk is None:
                break
            start, end = chunk
            for i in range(start, end):
                item_bytes = items[i].encode("utf-8")
                h1, h2 = self._hash(item_bytes)
                for j in range(K_HASHES):
                    bit_index = self._hash_single(h1, h2, j)
                    byte_index = bit_index >> 3
                    mask = 1 << (bit_index & 7)
                    lock = self._stripe_locks[byte_index % NUM_LOCK_STRIPES]
                    with lock:
                        self.bit_array[
                            byte_index
                        ] |= mask

    threads = [threading.Thread(target=worker) for _ in range(NUMBER_OF_THREADS)]
    for t in threads:
        t.start()
    for t in threads:
        t.join()
        \end{lstlisting}
        It's worth noting that we must use locks in \textit{add\_from\_file} to accurately represent the C++ version (it mimics the behavior of the \textit{\#pragma omp atomic update} directive) and to ensure safe write operations on a shared memory space.
        Locks are also used in the \textit{get\_next\_chunk} method to guarantee that the chunks processed by the threads remain independent and free of race conditions.
        \begin{lstlisting}[language=python]
def contains_from_file(self, items: list[str]) -> int:
    chunk_size = DYNAMIC_CHUNK_SIZE

    def process_chunk(chunk):
        count = 0
        for item in chunk:
            if self.contains(item):
                count += 1
        return count

    chunks = [items[i : i + chunk_size] for i in range(0, len(items), chunk_size)]
    with ThreadPool(processes=NUMBER_OF_THREADS) as pool:
        results = pool.map(process_chunk, chunks)
    return sum(results)
        \end{lstlisting}
        This was the most challenging part of the assignment, because, to guarantee consistency and comparability between the two implementations, we were constrained to mimic the C++ implementation. This forced us to use strategies, structures and techniques that are sub-optimal and not the best course of action in Python.
