        As expected, the average \textbf{TTC} (Time To Complete) of the parallel \textit{add} operation is significantly lower than the \textbf{TTC} of the sequential operation. However, since the two \textbf{TTC} are relatively small values, the resulting \textbf{speedup} and \textbf{efficiency} are rather low, especially the \textbf{efficiency}. The reason for this sub-optimal efficiency is caused by the \textit{\#pragma omp atomic update} directive, which enforces thread synchronization and degrades overall performance; but this is still a noticeable improvement over the sequential operation.
        Since \textit{contains} doesn't have any kind of synchronization among threads we can clearly see a sizable improvement in performance, having both \textbf{speedup} and \textbf{efficiency} at an acceptable level. 
        We can also observe that our theoretical prediction regarding false positives holds true.

