        \begin{table}[H]
            \centering
            \caption{Performances Average}
            \label{tab:avg_performance_python}
            \begin{tabular}{l cccc}
                \toprule
                \textbf{Operation} & \textbf{Avg. Seq. Time (s)} & \textbf{Avg. Par. Time (s)} & \textbf{Avg. Speedup} & \textbf{Avg. Efficiency} \\
                \midrule
                ADD      & 84.992587 & 83.825540 & 1.03$\times$ & 12.83\% \\
                CONTAINS &  2.669630 &  1.100622 & 2.44$\times$ & 30.53\% \\
                \bottomrule
            \end{tabular}
        \end{table}
        We would have expected, from the beginning, that the performances of the Python version would have been worse than its C++ counterpart. And we were right, by itself the \textbf{TTC} of the sequential versions of the operations 80 times slower then their C++ counterparts, but the most interesting find is that the parallel version of the operations is much slower than the sequential version. As said before, this massive degradation in performance is caused by the use of locks in the methods \textit{get\_next\_chunk} and \textit{add\_from\_file}, then adding the overhead of the Python language and the overhead of thread management, it becomes clear to see why the performances are the way they are. To improve the performances it would be needed a complete redesign of the structures, strategies and methods used, but this would mean departing from the C++ implementation making the two implementations no longer directly comparable. We still tried, to optimize the script, but, despite an improvement in the performances, the \textbf{TTC}s, speedups and efficiencies remained tens of times grater that the C++ implementation. We can assume that Python is not a suitable language for CPU-bound multithreading compared to C++.
        The prediction on the number of false positives still holds in this implementation.
